ving been cut (masc. sing.), having been cut (fem. sing.), having been cut (neut. sing.).\par }{\f208\cf17 \par }{\f208 NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Lign}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 rius, a, um, }{\f208 adj., }{\i\f211 relating to wood}{\f208 ; wood itself is }{\b\f210 l}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 gnum, }{\f208 -}{\b\f210 i}{\b\i\f212 , }{\f208 n.\par }{\b\f210 2. Insc}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 tus}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 a}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 um}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 ignorant}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 skilless}{\f208 . Derived from }{\b\f210 sc}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , -i}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to know}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 3. S}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rr\u225\'87 mech}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nic\u225\'87}{\f208 : ablative, complement of means; note that there is no preposition.\par }{\b\f210 4. St}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 pid\u233\'8e}{\f208  is an adverb, formed from }{\b\f210 st}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 pidus}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 a}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 um}{\f208 . }{\b\f210 Sec}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 erit}{\f208  is a perfect subjunctive (of }{\b\f210 sec}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to cut}{\f208 ), required by the conjunction }{\b\f210 ut}{\f208 ; we shall come back to this later.\par }{\b\f210 5. A m}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 dico}{\f208 : it is an ablative, complement of agent (vid. sup. N 3). But this time we are dealing a person and no longer with a thing: in this case we put in the prepopition }{\b\f210 a}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 by}{\f208 ).\par }{\b\f210 6. D}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mo}{\f208 :}{\b\f210  }{\f208 u}{\b\f210 nde }{\f208 e}{\b\f210 xis}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 from where do you come out?}{\f208  E}{\b\f210 xeo d}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 mo}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I come out of the house}{\f208 . }{\b\f210 R}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 deo Mass}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 li\u225\'87}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I return from Marseilles}{\f208 . The answer to the question u}{\b\f210 nde?}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 from where?}{\f208 , is put in the ablative without a preposition when we are dealing with a city or with the word }{\b\f2